###  https://w3id.org/cco-gro/data#CAN_UNIT_00072_condition_eligibility
data:CAN_UNIT_00072_condition_eligibility rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                   cco:Condition ;
                                          rdfs:label "Full-Time Program Eligibility Criteria" ;
                                          prov:wasDerivedFrom data:CAN_UNIT_00072 ;
                                          cco:hasConditionExpression "The full-time program may have breaks in study so long as they do not exceed 10% of the total study period for the program. In addition, no break can be longer than two consecutive weeks. Full-time course load criteria: a. For academic programs, the program must have sufficient required credits per quarter/semester/term/academic year to meet the definition of a full-time course load as defined by the institution. Note that not all institutions use the same credit system. b. Full-time status for non-credit career training programs of study is determined by the number of instructional hours per week as established by the institution. The absolute minimum number of hours of study for a full-time program in a post-secondary institution is 20 instructional hours per week and 15 instructional hours per week for aviation programs." .


###  https://w3id.org/cco-gro/data#CAN_UNIT_00072_condition_exception
data:CAN_UNIT_00072_condition_exception rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                 cco:Condition ;
                                        rdfs:label "Exception Conditions for Breaks" ;
                                        prov:wasDerivedFrom data:CAN_UNIT_00072 ;
                                        cco:hasConditionExpression "with the following exceptions: 4. a. Calendar year-end break, which can be up to three weeks in length; and b. Breaks immediately before or after a period of work-integrated learning during academic credit-based programs (funding will only be provided for the period the student is in work-integrated learning plus up to two weeks of break)" .


